rationales={
'jesus-arteaga':'His selective left-handed bat and receiving/throwing foundation provide a more balanced offensive-catching route than Joh’s power-led corner profile. Quintero remains ahead because of the contact/approach infield pathway and lighter catching burden. Joh’s impact is attractive, but Arteaga has the fuller two-sided evaluation in the recovered scouting.',
'joh-sung-chul':'The reported right-handed raw power and dated Raon High School game sample give a clearer impact hypothesis than Lías’s older amateur contact/speed evidence. Arteaga stays ahead because the catcher’s selective bat and defensive foundation are more comprehensively described. The 79-PA sample and reported KBO first-round interest support investigation, not an equivalent MLB draft grade; contact against better pitching and a likely corner role make this a low-confidence placement.',
'cristian-oscar-lias-bieneme':'Contact, running and a possible center-field home offer more category pathways than Dylan Rodriguez’s catching-led outcome. Joh narrowly leads on more recent competitive evidence of offensive impact, although the leagues and samples are not directly comparable. Lías’s youth results and academy reporting support a foundation rather than a measured professional hit tool.',
'dylan-rodriguez':'Feel for contact and bat speed with a viable catching path lead my comparison with Raider’s more one-dimensional present evidence. Lías stays ahead on the contact/speed/center-field combination. Raider may have greater raw power, but Rodriguez has a more balanced route to reaching useful game offense; neither player receives a guaranteed defensive outcome.',
'raider-sanchez-trutie':'A publicly described carrying power tool and Romero’s primary account of substantial scouting interest move him above Carrasco’s less differentiated fantasy impact. Rodriguez remains ahead because his contact foundation and possible catching contribution offer a fuller pathway. Raider is 20 and his reported 60–65 raw power is attributed opinion, with contact, speed and defense insufficiently established; I do not translate workout attention into a hit grade or use unsigned status as a talent penalty.',
'axel-carrasco':'His switch-hitting balance, reported emerging power and infield instincts remain preferable to Hernandez’s more contact-sensitive power bet. Raider narrowly leads on a more distinctive potential carrying power contribution, though Raider’s evaluation is less complete. Carrasco’s defensive possibilities are paths to playing time rather than an assumed utility floor.',
'eddy-hernandez':'The reported in-game impact and infield possibilities remain preferable to Álvarez Landa’s less recently tested offensive projection. Carrasco stays ahead because the more balanced contact/impact pathway gives his power more routes into games. Hernandez’s body size and reported bonus do not drive his position.',
'william-alejandro-alvarez-landa':'The selective amateur foundation and later physical impact place him ahead of Calderón’s less quantified offensive record. Hernandez retains the edge on more current evidence of reaching game damage. Álvarez Landa still needs recent competitive contact evidence; academy reports do not establish a professional grade.',
'anthony-calderon':'Reported power, arm strength and advanced catching defense form a more concrete two-sided case than Morua’s smaller, less comparable European sample. Álvarez Landa narrowly leads because his approach-and-impact foundation is better described offensively. Calderón’s exhibition home run is supporting context, not proof of a carrying bat; catcher development and contact uncertainty limit the placement, independently of the reported bonus or Braves destination.',
'marko-morua':'I favor the catching/arm foundation and inspectable international amateur background over De La Cruz’s more timing-dependent bat. Calderón stays ahead on more recent, explicit power and defensive reporting. The official European roster confirms catcher and right/right identity, while the father/coach interview adds development context; neither the short qualifier OPS nor Yankees interest establishes professional hitting ability. Signing-period disagreement remains separate from the talent comparison.',
'euniel-de-la-cruz':'Potential offensive impact keeps him ahead of Reyes’s contact-led profile with unresolved power. Morua narrowly leads on the more developed catching/arm case and documented competitive background, although competition and sample limitations make this close. De La Cruz’s moving parts are a repeatability question, not a measured contact failure.',
'wilson-reyes':'Left-handed contact, running and infield instincts give more developmental pathways than Morales’s less-tested outfield bat. De La Cruz remains ahead on more clearly described impact upside. Reyes’s reported small strikeout frequency is not treated as a verified rate, and the Rockies connection gives no ranking bonus.',
'kendry-morales':'The broad athletic outfield description remains preferable to Pérez Acuña’s more sparsely substantiated impact-and-catching claim. Reyes leads on the contact and infield pathway. Competitive hitting evidence is still needed; reported bonus and organization do not substitute for it.',
'sebastian-perez-acuna':'The attributed offensive and defensive potential gives a position-player upside case I narrowly prefer to Cruz’s incomplete pitching arsenal. Morales retains the edge on a more current, broader evaluation. Viral workout claims and star comparisons do not establish measured tools; contact and game-level catching evidence remain thin.',
'edenis-cruz':'A reported 91–94 mph fastball, strike-zone control and a dated Cuban youth innings record give a firmer pitching foundation than Erick González’s more projection-led present case. Pérez Acuña narrowly leads on potential carrying position-player value, despite lower evidence confidence. Cruz’s secondaries, pitch shapes and durable starter workload remain unverified; the youth ERA and single U18 outing cannot establish a professional starter ceiling.',
'erick-gonzalez-nicaragua-2027':'His described repertoire and projection give a more inspectable upside hypothesis than Yonewrys García’s fastball/control-only public report. Cruz moves ahead on the reported fastball band and larger historical competitive pitching record. Neither pitcher receives an assumed rotation role or invented pitch grades.',
'yonewrys-garcia':'The attributed strike-throwing praise and fastball up to 91 mph provide a clearer present carrying-skill hypothesis than Luis García’s limited offensive evidence. Erick González stays ahead because more of his repertoire and projection are described. Trainer/agent comments are not independently measured command, and secondary pitches, workload and nationality remain unverified; low information means low confidence, not a fixed low ceiling.',
'luis-garcia-nicaragua-2027':'Senior domestic experience and positional training give more inspectable context than Enyel González’s current tool record. Yonewrys narrowly leads because the specific fastball/control report gives a clearer route to a potentially useful skill. Brief batting results are not translated across leagues into a carrying hit tool.',
'enyel-gonzalez':'The shortstop and youth international background offer a positional pathway I narrowly prefer to Blanco’s currently incomplete workout-only arsenal. Luis García remains ahead on a more inspectable developmental record. The placement is low confidence because no well-supported carrying offensive tool has yet emerged from the accessible reports.',
'alayn-daniel-blanco':'The reported tall frame and fastball above 90 mph justify a prospect position, but they do not yet describe a complete pitching outcome. Enyel narrowly leads on the positional and youth competitive context; Blanco’s secondaries, strike throwing and workload are too sparsely documented to prefer him today. This is the widest information gap on the board, not a judgment that his undiscovered tools are poor, and unsigned/eligibility status is not assigned a numerical penalty.'}
